editorial:
## Method and reasoning
Cancel pairs of different values. Each cancellation removes at most one copy of a true strict majority and cannot destroy its majority among the remaining values. A zero balance lets you pick a new candidate. Finally count the candidate in the original array because cancellation can leave a candidate even when no majority exists.

## Walk through the sample
Pair one 7 with 1 and another 7 with 2. Two copies of 7 survive. Verification counts four copies in six positions, strictly above three. On [1,2,3] a surviving candidate would fail that same check.

## Complexity
Two scans take O(n) time. The candidate and balance use O(1) working space. The reference stores O(n) input values for the verification pass.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<int> a(n);
    int candidate = 0, balance = 0;
    for (int &x : a) {
        cin >> x;
        if (!balance)
            candidate = x;
        balance += x == candidate ? 1 : -1;
    }
    if (count(a.begin(), a.end(), candidate) > n / 2)
        cout << candidate << '\n';
    else
        cout << "NONE\n";
}
~~~
